@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:correctness] ground-is-the-rendered-surface
Every placed instance (trees, bushes, rocks, props, buildings, signs, paths) MUST be seated on the surface
that is rendered and collided at its point: the final stage of the product that owns that footprint. It
MUST NOT sample a natural, pre-carve, hidden, superseded, coarse or neighbouring surface, nor the max/min of
several. Where products overlap, the footprint's owner is the ground. An instance MUST rest within its
declared embedding of that surface: never floating above it and never buried beyond the embedding.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:correctness] seamless-surface-boundaries
Where two ground or walkable surfaces abut edge to edge (region and region, fine terrain and backdrop, city
and mountains), the edge MUST have one owner. The other side MUST meet the owner's exact edge profile there,
fading over a declared margin, from the same function and samples, with no vertical step, slit or sliver.
The mismatch MUST be zero within float tolerance, or within a tolerance named by that seam's own contract.
A coarse surface MUST carry vertices or knots where the fine edge bends. Where one surface lies over
another (paving, a deck or a slab over ground), the upper one owns the footprint, and the lower one MUST
stay below it by a declared clearance, never poking through. A skirt, apron or wall MAY close an intended
height change but MUST NOT hide an unexplained mismatch. A new or moved abutting boundary MUST add a test
that samples both sides along the whole edge against that tolerance.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:correctness] closed-visible-geometry
Geometry that a reachable camera can see from more than one side MUST be a closed solid, a slab with
thickness, or (where a thin surface is intended, as with cloth, foliage cards, flags or decals) a
two-sided material. That covers roofs, eaves, awnings, canopies, decks, bridges, stairs, balconies, signs
and walls seen from below or behind. A one-sided sheet is allowed only where no reachable viewpoint sees
its back. Roofs MUST seat on their walls with no gap, with soffits and gable infill closing them from below.
Intended openings (windows, doorways, open pavilions, bowls) are fine. Their edges MUST be finished with a
reveal, frame or thickness, so that no back face shows. Faces MUST wind outward consistently.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:correctness] pieces-meet-by-construction
Adjoining built pieces MUST meet by construction. That covers treads and risers, landings, walls and roofs,
paving and kerbs, platforms and their skirts, bridges and abutments, and modules and their neighbours.
Their shared edges MUST come from one plan or shared coordinates, not from separately tuned offsets: no
open gap, floating slab, daylight under a step or nosing, or coplanar overlap that flickers. A piece that
stands on the ground MUST reach below the ground it stands on, wherever that ground is lower.
In games/yorimichi_v2, editor-authored joins MAY instead be placed by hand: there an automated gap and
overlap check of the saved actors, not construction, accepts the join (yorimichi-v2-joins-checked).

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:correctness] walkable-routes-traversable
Every route the player is meant to walk (stairs, ramps, paths, landings and their joins to streets, terraces
and doors) MUST be traversable by the character on foot, in both directions, without jumping. Skate-only
features such as bowls and ramps are not walking routes. Risers MUST be at most 0.3 m, against the
character's 0.45 m step (UE MaxStepHeight; Yorimichi's train/collision.py STEP_UP). Treads MUST be at least a footstep
deep. Slopes MUST stay under the walkable floor angle. There MUST be no gap or lip at either end. A new or
geometrically changed route MUST be validated by driving the character over it both ways in a guarded game:
a committed scenario or a recorded drive through the live bridge (live.drive, UYorimichiLive::Drive), bound
to its source revision. Ray traces or plan checks alone are not enough.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:correctness] visual-defects-get-regression-checks
A fix for a reported visual or traversal defect (hole, slit, gap, floating or buried object, invisible
face, impassable step) MUST add an automated check that fails on the original defect. Where possible the
check is written against the general rule above it (a seam sampler, a grounding check, a closure check),
so that it also covers the next region or feature of the same kind. Where no automated check is feasible
(a pure material or art change), the PR MUST say why and attach revision-bound captures. Evidence MUST name
the source revision it ran on.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:architecture] one-implementation-per-mechanism
A shared world mechanism MUST have one implementation that every region reuses rather than copies. That
covers terrain products and queries, seam stitching, grounding, mesh builders and their closed primitives,
stairs and paving, foliage placement and LOD export. Existing region-local copies MUST be consolidated when
they are next changed. A region MUST keep its own data, layout and generators in its own region directory
(Yorimichi: games/yorimichi/world/regions/<region>/; Yorimichi V2 regions are ownership scopes, see
yorimichi-v2-single-writer). It MUST change another region's output only through
that region's published products or declared boundary APIs, never by patching its code paths, meshes,
materials or placements from outside. A ground function MUST serve every consumer of that ground (mesh,
collision and placement). A check MAY recompute independently as an oracle, but MUST NOT replace the
shared function.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:performance] edits-rebuild-locally
An edit to one region, asset or feature MUST rebuild only the products that actually read it. This rule
governs generating build graphs; it does not apply to games/yorimichi_v2, whose normal build generates
nothing (yorimichi-v2-targeted-authoring). Steps MUST
declare their own code, the shared modules and products they read, and spatial queries for neighbour reads,
following docs/WORLD_BUILD_PRACTICES.md. They MUST NOT declare whole directories, other regions' code or
global catalogues they do not consume. Terrain consumers MUST read published terrain products or narrow
kernels rather than importing a whole generator, so that unrelated work does not change their keys.
Procedural generation MUST be deterministic, with stable IDs and randomness seeded per region, asset and
purpose, so that unchanged inputs reproduce identical outputs. External and AI-generated outputs are not
regenerated: they are pinned, committed or cached inputs, recorded through atelier.ai.ledger. A recipe
change that widens the rebuild scope MUST report its measured effect (atelier build --changes and
--timings) in its PR.
